        { name: "Air Force Base Economy & Military Community", tag: "lean-into", why: "Altus Air Force Base is the economic anchor of southwestern Oklahoma's Jackson County area — federal employment, military family households, and service-sector activity that supports the district's $68,689 median income. Base retention, quality-of-life improvements for military families, and the policy conditions that make Altus AFB a preferred assignment are direct constituent interests where a state legislator's advocacy can reinforce federal-level investment. The history and scale of Altus AFB also explains the 8.65% Black community — above the western Oklahoma average — reflecting the historically significant African American military presence at this installation." },
        { name: "Agricultural Economy & Irrigation Water Rights", tag: "lean-into", why: "Jackson County is southwestern Oklahoma's cotton and grain sorghum belt — irrigation-intensive agriculture in a semi-arid climate where water rights, commodity prices, and farm credit are the economic fundamentals for the farming and ranching community. Agricultural infrastructure, rural road investment, and the water law framework that governs semi-arid row-crop farming are the practical governance issues for a constituency whose income and cultural identity are built on land. Water policy in southwestern Oklahoma is not an environmental issue — it is an existential agricultural one." },
        { name: "Southwestern Oklahoma Tribal Nations", tag: "careful", why: "The four reported demographic categories account for roughly 87% of the population — the ~13% AIAN gap reflects southwestern Oklahoma's tribal nations, including the Comanche Nation, Kiowa Tribe, Fort Sill Apache, and Caddo Nation, whose territories and citizenry extend into the Jackson County area. Indian Health Service access, tribal economic development, and the state-tribal relationship on gaming and government services are not abstract policy positions in this geography — they are the conditions under which a substantial share of households access healthcare and economic opportunity. An 81% margin does not constitute governing these communities." },
        { name: "Black Community Constituent Service", tag: "careful", why: "At 8.65%, HD-63's Black community is well above the western Oklahoma average and reflects both the legacy military community at Altus AFB and long-established civilian families. Economic mobility, healthcare access, housing stability, and public safety are their constituent concerns — and they are not captured in an 81% Republican result that does not require competing for their votes. A representative who builds authentic relationships with this community is building the durable service record that makes a dominant margin truly durable." },
        { name: "Confusing an 81% Margin for Constituent Satisfaction", tag: "avoid", why: "An 81% Republican result in a district that is 38% non-White and has an above-average Black population reflects the structural alignment of rural southwestern Oklahoma, not universal satisfaction with governance. Primary challenges are the more realistic threat to an incumbent here, and they come from the agricultural and homeowner base that evaluates whether legislative seniority is translating into tangible policy deliverables — water rights, base funding, rural infrastructure — not from the communities whose votes are not currently contested." }
